          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My mother has heart failure.",
          "answer": "Heart failure is a long-term condition in which the heart cannot pump or fill with blood as well as the body needs. The name can sound like the heart has stopped, but it has not. It is usually managed over many years. (Source: *Heart failure*, general clinical reference.)\n\nDoctors often classify it by ejection fraction, the share of blood the heart's main pumping chamber pushes out with each beat (*Heart failure*, general clinical reference):\n\n- **Reduced:** 40% or below.\n- **Mildly reduced:** 41% to 49%.\n- **Preserved:** 50% or above. This is about half of cases. It is not a milder form. The heart's problem is stiffness and poor filling rather than weak pumping.\n\nFor medical records and billing, heart failure is coded I50 in ICD-10-CM. The code depends on the type (systolic, diastolic, or both) and whether it is acute, chronic, or both (*Coding heart failure: I50*, US clinical modification of ICD-10).\n\nFor supporting your mother, the sources I searched say the following:\n\n- **Daily weight:** A sudden rise in weight can signal fluid build-up. Many people are asked to weigh themselves daily and contact their care team when it changes. (*Diet and fluid in heart failure*)\n- **Salt and fluid:** Limiting sodium and fluid is a common part of management. (*Diet and fluid in heart failure*)\n- **Alcohol:** It is generally limited or avoided, since it can weaken heart muscle. (*Diet and fluid in heart failure*)\n- **Supplements and salt substitutes:** Potassium-based products can interact with heart failure medicines, so they should be discussed with her clinician first. (*Diet and fluid in heart failure*)\n- **Action plan:** If she has one, it is the authority on what to watch for and whom to call. (*Diet and fluid in heart failure*)\n\nMy search did not find guidance on emotional support, caregiver roles, or how to help with appointments and medicines, so I can't add to those from the sources available. Her care team or a heart failure nurse would be the best place to ask. If you want, I can look up more on the daily weight or fluid guidance.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
