          "question": "Why does she have to weigh herself every day?",
          "answer": "Daily weighing is commonly recommended in heart failure because weight is an early sign of fluid build-up. The heart may not be pumping well enough to move fluid through the body, so fluid can collect in the lungs and legs. Weight usually rises before breathlessness or swelling appears. A gain of a few pounds over two or three days, or a larger gain over a week, is treated as fluid rather than tissue. Weighing each morning on the same scale, after using the bathroom and before eating, makes the trend easy to spot. (*Diet and fluid in heart failure*; *Heart failure*, general clinical reference)\n\nYour mother's care team may ask her to do this because she has heart failure. The advice can differ for other conditions or for people taking water pills (diuretics), so I can't say for certain that it applies in the same way to her. Do you know which condition her team has diagnosed? I could not find guidance specific to diuretic therapy in the sources I searched.\n\n**Rapid weight gain needs prompt medical advice.** If her weight rises quickly over a few days, she should contact her care team the same day. Her heart failure action plan, if she has one, will say whom to call. Seek emergency care right away if she has breathlessness at rest, breathlessness that wakes her at night, chest pain, fainting, or pink frothy spit with severe breathlessness. (*Heart failure*, general clinical reference)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: condition-heart-failure, lifestyle-heart-failure-diet]",
